</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The eight largest single day moves this month each coincided with a specific headline about the US Iran talks rather than a documented change in supply. Source: Investing.com historical futures data.</p><h2>What the Research Says About the Fix</h2><p>The corrective is not to ignore headlines. It is to separate the vividness of a claim from its evidentiary weight, which is precisely what a base rate forces an investor to do. Terrance Odean''s research on individual investor trading behaviour found that the accounts trading most frequently around news events were also the accounts with the worst net returns, a finding that has held across multiple market cycles since. The frequency of trading correlated with the frequency of headlines, not with any improvement in the information available.</p><p>None of this means the underlying conflict is resolved or that oil prices have found a floor. It means the size and direction of the next move is far less predictable from the headline alone than the headline itself suggests, and the investors most exposed to a costly decision right now are the ones treating today''s good foundation as meaningfully different from the 38 that came before it.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div><p>Clients with exposure to energy producers or gold miners are reacting to each individual headline rather than to a stable view of the underlying conflict. The swings feel significant even when the dollar amount is modest, because the pattern of reversal and renewed escalation has now repeated for nearly four months.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div><div class="script-box">I want to walk through what has actually happened to oil and gold this month, not just what the headlines said. Crude has moved more than 3 percent in either direction on eleven of the past twenty four trading sessions, and each of those moves lined up with a specific claim about the US Iran talks, not a documented change in how much oil is reaching the market. The research on this is clear: the most recent headline always feels like the most important one, even when it is not. Your portfolio is built around your actual goals and time horizon, not around this week''s headline. Here is what I would suggest we do, and what I would suggest we do not do, before our next call.</div></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div><p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with concentrated positions in TSX energy or gold mining names who have been adjusting those positions around news cycles.</p><p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> balanced portfolio holders with modest commodity sleeve exposure, where the swings show up in statements but not in financial outcomes.</p><p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients holding cash or short term fixed income who have been waiting for a clearer entry point.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div><div class="checklist-item">Review account level trading activity for the past month for trades placed within 24 hours of an Iran related headline.</div><div class="checklist-item">Flag any client who has made more than two such trades for a direct conversation.</div><div class="checklist-item">Pull the gold and energy sector weight for each affected household against its target allocation.</div><div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation, including the specific data points shared.</div></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div><div class="email-box" id="respond-email"><strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on this month''s oil and gold swings<br><br>Hi [Client Name],<br><br>Following up on our call. Crude oil has moved more than 3 percent in a single session eleven times this month, and each of those moves tracked a specific headline about the US Iran negotiations rather than an actual change in supply. Gold miners saw something similar on Friday, with Wheaton Precious Metals down 4.8 percent and Agnico Eagle down 2 percent as gold pulled back from its spring highs.<br><br>None of this changes the plan we built together. I would rather you hear the pattern from me than feel like you need to react to the next headline on your own.<br><br>[Your Name]<br><br><em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em></div><button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button></div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div><p>DIY investors holding concentrated energy or gold mining positions who have been trading around Iran headlines without a documented plan, and who have no one to call when the next reversal hits.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div><div class="script-box">I noticed oil moved more than 8 percent in a single session in May and almost 7 percent the other way a few weeks later. I am curious how you have been handling that kind of swing in your own portfolio.</div></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div><p>Self directed investors holding commodity exposure right now are making decisions in real time, headline by headline, with no framework for separating a genuinely new fact from a repeated claim. An advisor''s value in this specific environment is not predicting the next move in the Iran talks. It is having already built a portfolio that does not require predicting it, and having the discipline to point out when a client''s recent trades are tracking the news cycle rather than their own plan.</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div><p>How have you been deciding when to adjust your energy or gold positions this year?</p><p>Has the pace of the Iran headlines changed how often you check your portfolio?</p><p>What would it take for you to feel comfortable not reacting to the next headline?</p><p>If oil moved another 5 percent tomorrow, would you know what you would do, and why?</p></div><div class="toolkit-section"><div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div><div class="email-box" id="prospect-email"><strong>Subject:</strong> The pattern behind oil''s swings this month<br><br>Hi [Name],<br><br>WTI crude has moved more than 3 percent in a single session eleven times in the past month, with an 8.82 percent drop on May 20 and a 5.49 percent jump on June 1. Each move tracked a specific headline about the US Iran negotiations rather than a documented change in supply.<br><br>If you are managing your own exposure to energy or gold right now, I would welcome a short conversation about how you are separating the signal from the noise.<br><br>[Your Name]<br><br><em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em></div><button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button></div>',
  '[{"value": "-8.82%", "label": "WTI''s worst session this month"}, {"value": "38", "label": "Trump deal-imminent claims since March"}, {"value": "-4.8%", "label": "Wheaton Precious Metals'' Friday decline"}, {"value": "11/24", "label": "Sessions with 3%+ daily moves"}]',
