svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx:lastX, cy:lastY, r:4, fill:"#4a5568"}));
svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x:pillX, y:pillY, width:pillW, height:pillH, rx:3, fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:pillX+pillW/2, y:pillY+pillH/2+4, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"9", "font-weight":"700", fill:"#111111", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, "3% NOW"));
_cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Canada Revenue Agency prescribed interest rate notices, Q4 2022 to Q3 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each step reflects a CRA quarterly announcement; the rate is derived from average three month Government of Canada Treasury bill yields in the first month of the prior quarter. Source: Canada Revenue Agency prescribed interest rate notices, 2022 to 2026.</p><h2>What to Watch Before October</h2><p>None of this means the rate will rise in the fourth quarter. T-bill yields could hold steady or fall through July regardless of what the Fed signalled in June, and the Bank of Canada''s own posture remains far more cautious than its American counterpart''s. But the planning bridge does not require certainty about Q4 to be useful now. A client who has been meaning to set up a prescribed rate loan, or who has additional investable capital that could go through an existing family trust, has a known, locked in rate available today and an unresolved input pointing toward the possibility that today''s rate is the better one to lock in.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who already have a prescribed rate loan in place do not need to feel anything urgent. Their rate was locked at inception and this announcement does not touch it. The more relevant feeling to watch for is in clients with a Canadian controlled private corporation, who may notice the pertinent loan rate moved and wonder whether something has shifted underneath them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Your existing loan is unaffected. The rate you locked in when we set up the structure stays with that loan for as long as it exists, no matter what the CRA announces this quarter or next. The one number that did move in this release is the rate that applies to corporate shareholder loans, which ticked up slightly for the first time in two years. It is a small move, but it tells us the input behind the family lending rate may be starting to shift too. If you have additional capital sitting in a non-registered account that has not gone through the existing structure, this quarter is worth a conversation before the rate resets in October.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> business owner clients with a CCPC and an existing shareholder loan tied to the pertinent loan rate, which already moved this quarter.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> clients with an existing prescribed rate loan or family trust structure who hold additional uncommitted investable capital outside it.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients with an existing structure and no additional capital to add, who require no action at all.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm the locked in rate and documentation date on every existing prescribed rate loan in the book.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with a CCPC and an outstanding shareholder loan affected by the pertinent loan rate increase.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with uncommitted non-registered capital who have an existing family trust or spousal loan structure already in place.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation and any decision to add capital before the Q4 rate reset.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your prescribed rate loan and this quarter''s CRA update<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our call. The CRA confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3 percent through September, which has no effect on the loan we already set up since your rate was locked at the time the loan was made. One related rate, the corporate pertinent loan rate, did move slightly this quarter, the first such move in two years. It is worth a short conversation if you have additional capital you would like to add to the existing structure before the rate resets again in the fourth quarter.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p>Higher earning individuals with a lower income spouse, common law partner or adult child, and non-registered investable assets currently sitting in the higher earner''s name with no income splitting structure in place.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the family lending rate stays at 3 percent for another quarter, the fifth in a row at that level. I am reaching out to a few clients and prospects because that rate has not been this low for this long since 2022, and there is a real chance it does not stay there.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A prospect with a non-registered account sitting in a high bracket name and a lower income spouse or adult child is paying more tax on that investment income than they need to, every single year, with no action required beyond setting up a properly documented loan. The window is not permanent. The rate is set quarterly from short term Treasury bill yields, those yields are already showing early signs of moving, and a loan locks in whatever rate is in effect on the day it is made for as long as the loan exists. An advisor''s value here is knowing the mechanism well enough to act on the data before the rate resets, not after.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you or your spouse have non-registered investments sitting in the higher income earner''s name?</p>
<p>Have you ever looked at a prescribed rate loan or family trust as a way to split investment income?</p>
<p>If a low cost way to move investment income into a lower tax bracket existed today, would you want to know about it before it changed?</p>
<p>Do you have a Canadian controlled private corporation with an outstanding shareholder loan?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A tax planning window that may not last past September<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The CRA confirmed this week that the rate used for family income splitting loans stays at 3 percent through September, the fifth straight quarter at that level. A related corporate lending rate moved higher for the first time in two years in the same release, which is usually an early signal for where the family rate is headed next.<br><br>
If you have investments sitting in a higher earner''s name with a lower income spouse or adult child nearby, this is worth a short conversation while the current rate is still available to lock in.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '[{"value": "3%", "label": "Prescribed rate''s fifth straight quarter"}, {"value": "6.3%", "label": "Corporate pertinent loan rate now"}, {"value": "7%", "label": "CRA''s rate on overdue tax"}, {"value": "Jan 30", "label": "Deadline for annual interest payment"}]',
  'tax-034.jpg',
  'A quiet quarter for the family lending rate carries a planning deadline of its own, set by a calculation few clients ever see. Photo: Higgsfield.',
  5,
  '2026-06-22T09:05:00',
  'entity:cra,entity:trust-structure,entity:prescribed-rate-loan,entity:fed,theme:fed-rate-path,stance:tail-risk-flag',
  1,
  'Compiled by HDQ (hdq.ca) from: Canada Revenue Agency prescribed interest rate notices, Q4 2022 through Q3 2026; Investment Executive; Advisor.ca; KRP LLP; LegalClarity; Department of Finance Canada lending rates page; Federal Reserve press release, June 17, 2026; Bank of Canada press release, June 10, 2026.'
);
