VALUES (
  '2026/06/22/fed-dot-plot-boc-policy-divergence-widens',
  'economy',
  'article',
  'The Fed''s Median Rate Path Just Jumped 40 Basis Points. The Bank of Canada''s Has Not Moved at All.',
  'Nine of eighteen Federal Reserve officials now see at least one more rate increase in 2026, a sharp reversal from March. The Bank of Canada, weighing a second straight quarterly contraction, held at 2.25 percent for a fifth consecutive meeting and faces its next decision July 15.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The Federal Reserve held its policy rate at 3.50 to 3.75 percent on June 17 in new Chair Kevin Warsh''s first meeting,</strong><span> but the accompanying dot plot showed nine of eighteen officials projecting at least one rate increase in 2026, up from a unanimous no hike projection in March.</span></li>
<li><strong>The median dot now implies a year end policy rate of 3.8 percent, up from 3.4 percent in March,</strong><span> one of the sharpest single meeting revisions to the Fed''s own rate path on record.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada held its policy rate at 2.25 percent on June 10 for a fifth consecutive decision,</strong><span> even as Governor Tiff Macklem acknowledged the economy contracted for a second straight quarter in the first three months of the year.</span></li>
<li><strong>Bond markets currently price roughly a 7 percent probability the Bank of Canada raises its rate at the July 15 decision,</strong><span> rising to about 30 percent by the September 2 meeting.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Canadian dollar traded near 0.7065 against the US dollar this week,</strong><span> within sight of its 52 week low, as the widening gap between the two central banks'' policy paths weighed on the currency.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Federal Reserve held its policy rate at 3.50 to 3.75 percent on June 17, exactly as markets expected heading into the meeting. The number that moved markets was not the decision itself. It was the dot plot, the quarterly summary of each official''s own projection for where the rate should sit by year end, and it showed a committee that has shifted meaningfully since March.</p><p>Of the eighteen officials who submitted a projection, nine now see at least one rate increase before the end of 2026. One projects a cumulative increase of 75 basis points. Five project 50 basis points. Three project 25 basis points. Eight see no change at all, and one still expects a 25 basis point cut. The resulting median dot implies a year end rate of 3.8 percent, up from the 3.4 percent the same committee projected in March, when not a single official had pencilled in a hike.</p><h2>What Changed Since March</h2><p>The mechanism is straightforward even if the politics around it are not. Newly confirmed Chair Kevin Warsh, who took over from Jerome Powell in May, ran his first meeting against a backdrop of a stronger than expected May jobs report and an energy price shock that pushed inflation to its highest level in three years. Warsh did not submit a dot of his own, citing his long standing objection to the exercise, but his press conference language was read by economists as unmistakably hawkish. He told reporters the Fed had missed its inflation target for five years and intended to fix that, language that Deutsche Bank''s Matthew Luzzetti said raised the visible risk of a hike rather than a cut.</p><p>Equity markets registered the shift immediately. The Nasdaq 100 fell roughly 1.3 percent on the day of the decision, and bond yields rose as traders repriced the odds of further easing lower. The transmission to Canada runs through two channels at once: a stronger US dollar that pressures the Canadian dollar directly, and a higher US rate path that pulls global short term yields, including Government of Canada Treasury bill yields, along with it.</p><h2>The Bank of Canada Is Reading a Different Economy</h2><p>The Bank of Canada held its own policy rate at 2.25 percent on June 10, the fifth consecutive hold and a decision economists had widely expected. Governor Tiff Macklem''s tone, however, leaned more cautious than hawkish. He acknowledged the Canadian economy contracted for a second straight quarter in the opening three months of the year, driven in part by US trade policy uncertainty and the now resolving war in Iran, while stopping short of calling it a recession. Core inflation, Macklem noted, has cooled in recent months even as the headline rate has been pushed up by energy costs, a distinction he said the Bank is watching closely.</p><p>That distinction is the whole story for the next decision. Bond markets currently price roughly a 7 percent probability of a Bank of Canada rate increase on July 15, rising to about 30 percent by the September 2 meeting, a pricing pattern that reflects genuine two way risk rather than a settled view. The CUSMA joint review deadline falls this same month, and an outcome that ratchets tariff levels higher or extends trade uncertainty into the back half of the year would weigh further on a Canadian economy that Macklem has already flagged as softer than expected.</p><p>Placed on the same scale, the Fed''s eighteen projections show a committee with a meaningfully higher centre of gravity than the unanimous, no hike consensus it held in March, while the Bank of Canada''s own rate has not moved in five consecutive decisions.</p><div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">FOMC YEAR END 2026 PROJECTIONS</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">3.8%</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#c0392b;">&#9650; MEDIAN, FROM 3.4%</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">18 PARTICIPANTS &nbsp;|&nbsp; JUN 17, 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
var _cs = document.currentScript;
function el(tag, attrs, txt){ var n = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", tag); for (var k in attrs){ n.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]); } if (txt !== undefined && txt !== null){ n.textContent = txt; } return n; }
var levels = [[4.375,1],[4.125,5],[3.875,3],[3.625,8],[3.375,1]];
var W = 680, H = 300, margin = {left:62, right:24, top:18, bottom:46};
var PW = W - margin.left - margin.right, PH = H - margin.top - margin.bottom;
var yMin = 3.2, yMax = 4.5;
function yS(v){ return margin.top + ((yMax - v)/(yMax - yMin))*PH; }
var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox", "0 0 680 300");
svg.setAttribute("width", "100%");
var yTicks = [3.375,3.625,3.875,4.125,4.375];
for (var t=0; t<yTicks.length; t++){
  var ty = yS(yTicks[t]);
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:ty, y2:ty, stroke:"#ececec", "stroke-width":"0.5"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:margin.left-6, y:ty+3, "text-anchor":"end", "font-size":"8.5", fill:"#aaa", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, yTicks[t]+"%"));
}
var medianY = yS(3.75);
svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1:margin.left, x2:margin.left+PW, y1:medianY, y2:medianY, stroke:"#888888", "stroke-width":"1", "stroke-dasharray":"3,3"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:margin.left+10, y:medianY-6, "text-anchor":"start", "font-size":"7.5", fill:"#888", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, "MARCH MEDIAN, 3.4% TO 3.75% TARGET MIDPOINT"));
var marchY = yS(3.375);
svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx:margin.left+40, cy:marchY-20, r:0, fill:"none"}));
var colCenter = margin.left + PW/2;
var dotSpacing = 16;
for (var li=0; li<levels.length; li++){
  var lvl = levels[li][0], count = levels[li][1];
  var y = yS(lvl);
  var totalWidth = (count-1)*dotSpacing;
  var startX = colCenter - totalWidth/2;
  for (var i=0;i<count;i++){
    var cx = startX + i*dotSpacing;
    var fillCol = lvl > 3.625 ? "#8a3030" : (lvl < 3.625 ? "#3a7a55" : "#4a5568");
    svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx:cx, cy:y, r:6, fill:fillCol, opacity:"0.85"}));
  }
  svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:colCenter+totalWidth/2+16, y:y+3, "text-anchor":"start", "font-size":"8", "font-weight":"700", fill:"#444", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, count+" PARTICIPANTS"));
}
var pillW = 100, pillH = 16;
var topLevelY = yS(4.375);
var pillX = colCenter - pillW/2;
var pillY = topLevelY - 22;
svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x:pillX, y:pillY, width:pillW, height:pillH, rx:3, fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:pillX+pillW/2, y:pillY+pillH/2+4, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"9", "font-weight":"700", fill:"#111111", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, "9 OF 18 SEE A HIKE"));
svg.appendChild(el("text", {x:colCenter, y:H-margin.bottom+24, "text-anchor":"middle", "font-size":"8", fill:"#999", "font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}, "YEAR END 2026 TARGET RANGE MIDPOINT"));
_cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Federal Reserve Summary of Economic Projections, June 17, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each dot represents one of eighteen Federal Open Market Committee participants'' year end 2026 rate projection, expressed as the midpoint of the implied target range. Chair Warsh did not submit a projection. Source: Federal Reserve Summary of Economic Projections, June 17, 2026.</p><h2>What This Means for the Loonie and for Renewals</h2><p>The Canadian dollar traded near 0.7065 against the US dollar this week, within sight of the 52 week low of 0.7053 set Friday, as the widening gap between the two central banks'' rate paths weighed on the currency alongside falling oil prices. A weaker loonie raises the cost of imported goods, a transmission channel the Bank of Canada will be watching against its own inflation mandate even as it holds its policy rate steady.</p><p>For now, the practical read for Canadian households is continuity rather than change. The Bank of Canada''s rate, and therefore variable mortgage and home equity line of credit pricing, remains at 2.25 percent with the next scheduled update July 15. The risk worth watching is not a Canadian rate increase next month. It is whether US policy continues to pull global yields higher through the summer, narrowing the room the Bank of Canada has to cut if the second quarter contraction Macklem flagged turns out to be the start of a pattern rather than a one quarter dip.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with variable rate mortgages or home equity lines feel a vague unease from the word hawkish without always connecting it to their own renewal. Clients who follow markets more closely may feel whiplash from a Fed that went from unanimous holds to a split committee in a single meeting, and want reassurance that the Bank of Canada is not about to do the same thing overnight.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Fed''s own internal forecast moved meaningfully this month, with about half of its officials now expecting at least one rate increase before year end, up from none in March. That is a US story first and a Canadian story second. The Bank of Canada held its rate steady for a fifth straight time on June 10, and the market is pricing only a small chance of a change at its next decision on July 15. Your mortgage and your portfolio are positioned for the environment we actually have here, not for whatever the headlines about Washington suggest.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with variable rate mortgages or HELOCs whose payments are directly tied to the Bank of Canada''s rate, and clients with significant US dollar exposure affected by a weaker loonie.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> clients with fixed income allocations who benefit from elevated yields on the US side but face currency translation effects on unhedged US holdings.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients renewing a mortgage this year who locked in expectations of further Bank of Canada cuts, and now have more clarity that the current rate is likely to persist.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review variable rate mortgage and HELOC clients for renewal dates between now and the September 2 Bank of Canada decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check unhedged US dollar fixed income or equity exposure against the recent move in the Canadian dollar.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag households with discretionary US dollar spending, such as travel or cross border shopping, for a brief currency conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation and any portfolio adjustments discussed.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the Fed''s shift means, and does not mean, for you<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following up on our call. The Federal Reserve''s internal projections shifted meaningfully this month, with about half its officials now expecting at least one more rate increase in 2026. The Bank of Canada, by contrast, held its own rate steady for a fifth consecutive decision on June 10 and is not expected to move much at its July 15 meeting. Your plan reflects the Canadian environment we are actually in, and I will flag anything that changes that.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p>DIY investors with significant unhedged US dollar exposure or a variable rate mortgage who have been following Fed headlines without a clear read on what they mean for their own, Canadian dollar denominated plan.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Fed''s own forecast for its rate jumped 40 basis points in a single meeting this month, while the Bank of Canada has not moved in five straight decisions. I am curious whether your current plan accounts for the fact that those two paths are now pulling in different directions.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A self directed investor tracking US financial media is absorbing a steady stream of Fed focused headlines that may have little direct bearing on their own, Canadian dollar denominated mortgage or portfolio. The value of an advisor in this specific moment is translation: knowing which US developments actually transmit into Canadian rates and the currency, and which ones do not, so a client is not making decisions based on the wrong central bank.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How much of your portfolio is in unhedged US dollar holdings right now?</p>
<p>Has the recent Fed news changed how you are thinking about your mortgage renewal?</p>
<p>Do you have a clear sense of how the Bank of Canada''s decisions, rather than the Fed''s, actually affect your own borrowing costs?</p>
<p>Would it help to walk through what is actually different between the US and Canadian rate paths right now?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Two central banks, two different stories right now<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The Federal Reserve''s own rate forecast jumped 40 basis points in a single meeting this month, while the Bank of Canada has held steady for five straight decisions. If you are managing a mortgage or a portfolio without a clear sense of which central bank actually matters most for your situation, I would welcome a short conversation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '[{"value": "3.8%", "label": "Fed''s new median year-end rate"}, {"value": "9/18", "label": "Fed officials projecting a hike"}, {"value": "2.25%", "label": "BoC''s rate after five holds"}, {"value": "0.7065", "label": "CAD versus the US dollar"}]',
  'economy-034.jpg',
  'Two central banks are now reading two different economies, and the gap between them is starting to show up in the currency. Photo: Higgsfield.',
  6,
  '2026-06-22T09:07:00',
  'entity:fed,entity:boc,entity:macklem,entity:cad,theme:fed-rate-path,theme:boc-rate-path,theme:cad-weakness,stance:base-case',
  1,
  'Compiled by HDQ (hdq.ca) from: Federal Reserve press release and Summary of Economic Projections, June 17, 2026; Bank of Canada press releases, April 29 and June 10, 2026; CNBC; PBS News; Yahoo Finance; Vantage Markets; Hiring Lab; BNN Bloomberg; nesto.ca Bank of Canada rate schedule; True North Mortgage rate forecast; Wise CAD/USD historical data.'
);
