<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily Brent settlement prices, May 22 to June 22, 2026. The shaded period covers the memorandum of understanding signing, the renewed Hormuz claim and the resumed Switzerland talks. Source: Investing.com historical futures data.</p><h2>What Advisors Should Watch Next</h2><p>The technical talks in Switzerland are scheduled to continue through the week, with a high level committee now in place to oversee the broader mediation process. Nothing about the 60 day roadmap requires a clean, linear path to a final deal, and another Hormuz claim or another flare up in Lebanon before the window closes would not be a surprise. What would be a surprise, on the evidence of this weekend, is the market reacting to the next one as sharply as it reacted to the first.</p><p>For Canadian portfolios with energy sector exposure, the practical implication is less about the next headline and more about whether producer guidance and dividend coverage assumptions have caught up to an 80 dollar world rather than a 100 dollar one.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with concentrated Canadian energy exposure heard a headline about Hormuz closing again and may be bracing for the kind of sharp move that the same headline produced earlier in the conflict. The more useful feeling to address is the gap between that expectation and what actually happened, which was very little.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Iran made the same closure claim about the Strait of Hormuz this weekend that it has made several times since this conflict started, and this time crude barely moved. The market has shipping data now that it did not have in March, tankers are actually moving through the strait, and that physical evidence is carrying more weight than the rhetoric. What matters more for your energy holdings right now is not the next headline out of the Middle East. It is whether the companies you own built their 2026 plans around oil staying near 100 dollars or whether they were already planning for something closer to where we are today.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with concentrated positions in Canadian oil producers whose realized prices and dividend coverage were modeled against a higher oil price assumption earlier this year.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> diversified equity holders with sector weight energy exposure roughly in line with the TSX composite.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients with energy sector underweights or refining and transportation exposure that benefits from lower input costs.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the oil price assumption embedded in any recent producer guidance held in client portfolios against Goldman''s revised 80 dollar fourth quarter forecast.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag concentrated energy positions for a dividend coverage conversation rather than a reaction to today''s headline specifically.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check whether any client trades this month were placed in direct response to a Hormuz headline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why oil barely moved on this weekend''s Hormuz headline<br><br>
