</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p>DIY investors with concentrated, self directed positions in Canadian energy producers who have been trading around Hormuz headlines all year and have not revisited whether their holdings'' guidance still matches the current oil price.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Brent crude is down more than 20 percent in the past month, and barely moved when Iran claimed to close the Strait of Hormuz again this weekend. I am curious whether the energy names in your portfolio were built around oil staying closer to 100 dollars.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>An investor holding concentrated Canadian energy positions through this conflict has likely been reacting to headlines about Hormuz all year, when the more durable risk was always the price assumption underneath the dividend and the guidance, not the headline of the week. An advisor''s value right now is in re-underwriting that assumption against where Brent actually trades, not in predicting the next geopolitical move.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How concentrated is your portfolio in Canadian energy producers right now?</p>
<p>Have you checked the oil price assumption behind the dividend guidance of the companies you hold?</p>
<p>How have you been deciding when to trade around Middle East headlines this year?</p>
<p>Would it help to walk through which of this year''s Hormuz claims actually changed anything?</p>
