<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily WTI crude settlement prices and trading volume, May 18 to June 18, 2026. Source: Investing.com historical futures data.</p><h2>What to Watch at the Open</h2><p>US equity futures were broadly flat to modestly higher Monday morning, with the S&P 500 and Dow both inching higher and the Nasdaq 100 up roughly 0.3 percent, as markets digested the weekend''s progress on the Iran talks alongside a shortened trading week behind them. The next real test for the Fed''s hawkish read is Thursday''s release of the May personal consumption expenditures price index, the central bank''s preferred inflation gauge, which will be the first hard data point markets have had since Chair Warsh''s press conference comments on inflation.</p><p>For Canadian portfolios, the open worth watching is less about direction and more about whether Friday''s pattern repeats: a market where the average sector move understates how much dispersion is happening underneath it.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with concentrated gold mining positions are looking at a sharp, uniform drop across their holdings and may feel singled out. Clients with energy exposure are seeing a confusing mix of one name up and another down on the same day, which can feel less like a market move and more like something specific to their own picks.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Friday''s move in gold miners was not specific to any one company. Gold itself fell to its lowest level in over a week because a stronger US dollar, driven by the Fed''s hawkish shift this month, weighs on every dollar priced commodity the same way. Every miner you hold felt that same pressure, which is exactly why they all moved together. Energy is a different story right now. Oil prices are sliding as the Iran situation calms down, but individual energy stocks are also responding to company specific news, which is why you are seeing names move in different directions on the same day. Neither pattern changes the plan we built together.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> clients with concentrated gold mining positions, which moved together and sharply lower on Friday.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> clients holding a basket of Canadian energy names, where the average return understates how differently individual holdings performed.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients with significant US dollar exposure, who benefit from the currency move weighing on the loonie.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review concentrated gold mining positions for clients who may be reacting to Friday''s uniform decline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check energy sector holdings for dispersion between names rather than assuming the sector moved as a block.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note any client US dollar exposure that benefits from the loonie''s move toward its 52 week low.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversation.</div>
</div>
